<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Admonitions of the Instructress to the Court Ladies</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Admonitions Scroll</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Admonitions of the Instructress to the Court Ladies is a Chinese handscroll painting illustrating a moralising text written by Zhang Hua in 292 for women of the imperial court. Traditionally attributed to the Eastern Jin painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gu-kaizhi">Gu Kaizhi</a>, the surviving scroll in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a> is probably a copy made in the fifth or sixth century. It is among the oldest surviving Chinese handscrolls and a key work of early figure painting.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/admonitions-of-the-instructress-to-the-court-ladies.jpg" alt="Admonitions of the Instructress to the Court Ladies" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gu Kaizhi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ku_K%27ai-chih_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
